ask(c,'Which operation best illustrates dimensionality reduction rather than assigning cluster membership?', ['Represent each high-dimensional scan by a shorter feature vector','Assign each scan to one of three terrain groups','Choose the nearest centroid and return its integer identifier','Separate sensor records into groups of similar readings'],['A shorter representation changes feature dimension rather than returning a group membership.','This is a clustering operation.','This is nearest-centroid cluster assignment.','This describes clustering.'],kind='scenario')
c=concept(811,'K-means flow, geometric update and visualization code','Initialize, alternate assignments and means, stop criterion, nearest distances and centroid markers.')
ask(c,'Which order follows the displayed K-means flowchart?', ['Initialize centers → assign nearest centers → update cluster means → repeat until centers stop changing','Initialize centers → update means before any memberships exist → assign nearest centers → stop','Initialize centers → assign nearest centers → replace each center by the farthest point → repeat','Initialize centers → assign nearest centers once → stop without a mean update'],['The loop alternates membership assignment and arithmetic-mean recomputation after initialization.','Cluster means require assigned members; this order tries to update before defining them.','The update uses arithmetic means, not the farthest point.','The displayed algorithm includes iterative mean updates rather than one fixed-center assignment.'],kind='sequence')
ask(c,'In a supplied one-dimensional K-means assignment, centers are c₀=2 and c₁=8 and a point is x=6. What is its nearest-center assignment?', ['Cluster 1 because squared distances are 16 and 4','Cluster 0 because squared distances are 16 and 4','Cluster 1 because squared distances are 4 and 16','Neither cluster because x is not itself a center'],['(6−2)²=16 and (6−8)²=4, so cluster 1 is closer.','The smaller squared distance is to center 1.','The chosen cluster is right but the stated ordered distances are reversed.','A point need not coincide with a center to be assigned.'],kind='numerical',formula=[DIST],steps=['Compute distances to c₀ and c₁: |6−2|=4 and |6−8|=2.','Square them to get 16 and 4.','Select the smaller score, cluster 1.'],assumptions=['One normalized feature; Euclidean distance; no tie.'])
ask(c,'A nonempty cluster contains normalized 2D points (0,0), (2,0), and (4,6). What center does the displayed mean-update rule produce?', ['(2,2)','(2,0)','(6,6)','(4,6)'],['The coordinate means are (0+2+4)/3=2 and (0+0+6)/3=2.','This uses coordinate medians instead of means.','This gives the coordinate sum rather than mean.','This selects a sample rather than computing the centroid.'],kind='numerical',formula=[DIST],steps=['Sum the first coordinates to get 6 and the second coordinates to get 6.','Divide each sum by three samples.','The new centroid is (2,2).'],assumptions=['The points are already assigned to the same nonempty cluster; coordinates are normalized.'])
ask(c,'What does the flowchart’s “centers remained unchanged” condition establish in its simplified exact-arithmetic setting?', ['A fixed point of the assignment/update loop, not by itself proof of the global optimum','A guarantee that every sample equals its centroid','A guarantee that all clusters contain equal numbers of points','A requirement to increase k before stopping'],['Unchanged centers stop this loop; the diagram does not establish global optimality.','Clusters can contain spread-out points around unchanged means.','The mean-update rule does not enforce equal membership counts.','The shown flow stops and returns k clusters without increasing k.'],difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Fixed data, no ties changing assignments and no empty clusters; simplified exact fixed-point stopping rule, not a claim about every library tolerance.'])
ask(c,'What is the purpose of the displayed call mglearn.plots.plot_kmeans_algorithm()?', ['Visualize steps of the K-means algorithm','Train the torque LinearRegression estimator on X_lifting and y_torque','Create the three Gaussian sensor arrays used on the later slide','Return the predicted zone for new_reading directly'],['The name and associated figure identify an algorithm-visualization helper.','The torque model is trained by regressor.fit, not this plot helper.','The synthetic arrays are generated by np.random.normal in separate code.','The later estimator’s predict method returns the zone.'],kind='code',assumptions=['Interpret the exact source call and its displayed output; execution requires the separate mglearn package.'])
ask(c,'In the K-means illustration, what do the colored triangles represent relative to the circular samples?', ['Cluster centers, with colors corresponding to memberships','The three newest sensor readings only','Known supervised target values for all samples','The three axes of a regression plane'],['The legend identifies the triangular center markers for clusters 0, 1 and 2.','The circles represent samples and the triangles represent their centers.','No supervised target values are shown.','This is a two-dimensional cluster plot, not a regression plane.'],kind='diagram')
c=concept(812,'Sensor-zone clustering problem','Feature pair, unlabeled setup, chosen cluster count and feature-space meaning.')
ask(c,'Which configuration matches the indoor zone-clustering problem?', ['Temperature in °C and Wi-Fi strength in percent; no labeled target; three requested groups','Temperature and torque; labeled room names; two requested groups','Wi-Fi strength only; known torque labels; three requested outputs','Mass and extension; no input measurements; three rewards'],['The source gives those two sensor features and asks for three zones without target labels.','Torque, room labels and two clusters are not the printed setup.','The temperature feature is present, and torque labels belong to the regression example.','Mass/extension belong to lifting, and clustering requires measured inputs.'],kind='match')
ask(c,'Does grouping similar temperature/Wi-Fi readings alone prove that each cluster is one physically connected room?', ['No; feature similarity is not by itself spatial connectivity or room identity','Yes; every cluster identifier is a measured geographic coordinate','Yes; choosing k=3 proves that the building contains exactly three rooms','No; clustering is impossible unless room names were supplied first'],['No spatial coordinates or room labels are supplied to establish that conclusion.','A cluster ID is an arbitrary membership label, not a coordinate.','The chosen number of groups does not discover a guaranteed room count.','Unsupervised clustering can proceed without known room names.'],kind='scenario')
c=concept(813,'Seeded Gaussian sensor-data generation','Imports, means, standard deviations, shape, simulation limits and seed scope.')
ask(c,'In np.random.normal([10,20],[2,5],(100,2)), what do [10,20] and [2,5] specify?', ['Per-feature means and standard deviations, respectively','Per-feature minima and maxima, respectively','Per-feature variances and means, respectively','The number of rows and columns, respectively'],['The first vector supplies location and the second scale for the temperature and Wi-Fi columns.','A normal distribution is not bounded by these vectors.','The second vector contains standard deviations, not the first vector’s means reversed.','The explicit (100,2) argument sets shape.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'Which mean pair corresponds to zone_B in the source’s synthetic generator?', ['22 °C and 90% Wi-Fi','10 °C and 20% Wi-Fi','25 °C and 40% Wi-Fi','23 °C and 85% Wi-Fi'],['The zone_B generation call uses [22,90].','These are the zone_A means.','These are the zone_C means.','These are the later test reading, not a generator mean.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'What does the (100,2) output shape mean for each generated zone?', ['100 observations, each with temperature and Wi-Fi features','Two observations, each with 100 temperature features','100 labeled clusters, each with two centroids','100 rows of training labels and two rows of test labels'],['Rows are observations and the two columns follow the supplied feature order.','This reverses sample and feature dimensions.','The shape does not declare cluster counts or centroid numbers.','The generator produces input features, not labels or train/test splits.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'What role does np.random.seed(42) play in the printed data-generation sequence?', ['It fixes the NumPy random generator’s starting state for reproducible draws under the same setup','It makes every generated observation equal to its mean','It specifies that exactly 42 samples must be drawn','It automatically fixes all later libraries’ random choices regardless of their settings'],['Repeating the same generator calls from the same state reproduces the synthetic array sequence in the same setup.','The samples still vary; the seed selects a reproducible random sequence.','Sample count comes from the shape, which is 100 rows per zone.','The KMeans estimator also has its own explicit random_state parameter.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'The slide writes temperature 10±2 and Wi-Fi 20±5 for the Gaussian example. Which interpretation is correct?', ['These describe mean and standard deviation, not guaranteed minimum and maximum values','Every temperature lies in [8,12] and every Wi-Fi value in [15,25]','The first number is always a variance and the second is a mean','The plus/minus values ensure Wi-Fi can never exceed 100% in any generated zone'],['Normal-distribution scale is a standard deviation; normal draws can lie beyond one standard deviation and beyond physical percentage bounds.','Those are one-standard-deviation intervals, not hard bounds.','The code passes means first and standard deviations second.','The normal model is unbounded; the plot even shows Wi-Fi samples above 100%.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[813,815],conflict=(813,'The annotation uses ± standard-deviation notation for simulated sensor values, which can be misread as hard physical ranges.','Interpret scale as standard deviation, not bounds; untruncated Gaussian percentage samples can exceed 100%, as the plotted source demonstrates.'))
issue(813,'gaussian-bounds','Gaussian synthetic Wi-Fi values are not constrained to [0,100]; ± annotations are standard deviations, not hard limits.','Explicitly treat this as a toy sensor distribution; preserve printed data and plot, including values above 100%.')
c=concept(814,'K-means estimator and raw feature scaling','Stacking, fit without y, chosen k, new-row prediction and units-sensitive distance.')
ask(c,'If zone_A, zone_B and zone_C each have shape (100,2), what does np.vstack([zone_A,zone_B,zone_C]) produce?', ['A (300,2) matrix preserving two features per observation','A (100,6) matrix merging all features side by side','A (3,100,2) array retaining a separate zone axis','A (300,) vector of scalar targets'],['Vertical stacking concatenates rows, giving 300 observations and two columns.','This would be horizontal concatenation, not vstack here.','This would retain a separate stack axis rather than concatenate rows.','The two feature columns are not removed.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'Why does clustering_model.fit(X_sensors) omit a y array in the shown code?', ['K-means estimates groups from the input features without target labels','K-means secretly uses the zone names as a required supervised target vector','The target is always the second feature column and is removed automatically','fit is only a plotting command and performs no learning'],['The source explicitly notes no output y array in this unsupervised setup.','The names of generation variables are not passed as training labels.','Wi-Fi remains an input feature; fit does not make it the supervised target here.','fit estimates the cluster model; plotting is separate.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'In KMeans(n_clusters=3, random_state=42), which role belongs to n_clusters=3?', ['Request three clusters before fitting','Ask the algorithm to discover the best number among exactly three candidate values','Specify three features per observation','Require exactly three update iterations'],['The parameter specifies the requested number of centers/groups.','There is no automatic model-selection sweep in the shown call.','X_sensors has two features, independent of the cluster count.','The stopping iterations are controlled separately, not by n_clusters.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'What does clustering_model.predict([[23,85]])[0] return in this example?', ['The cluster identifier assigned to one temperature/Wi-Fi observation','The two-feature centroid vector itself','The probability that the room is physically Zone 0','A new training target used to refit all centers immediately'],['predict returns one cluster label per input row, and [0] extracts this row’s label.','Centers are model parameters, not the returned label value.','This call does not return a calibrated semantic room probability.','Prediction assigns membership without refitting the centers.'],kind='code')
ask(c,'The source passes raw temperature and Wi-Fi values directly to Euclidean K-means. If temperature alone is rescaled from °C differences to hundredths of a degree, what may happen?', ['Assignments may change because the squared temperature contribution grows by 10,000 relative to unchanged Wi-Fi values','Assignments are guaranteed identical because Euclidean distance ignores coordinate scaling','Only the arbitrary text name of each cluster changes; distances cannot change','The number of observations automatically increases by a factor of 100'],['Multiplying one coordinate difference by 100 multiplies its squared-distance term by 100².','Euclidean distance depends on feature scale.','Relative distances can change, not merely output names.','Changing units does not add observations.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[DIST],assumptions=['Only the temperature coordinate is multiplied by 100; no standardization is added and Wi-Fi values are unchanged.'],steps=['Original temperature contribution is (ΔT)².','Rescaled contribution is (100ΔT)²=10,000(ΔT)².','Relative weighting changes, so nearest-center decisions can change.'])
issue(814,'implementation-scope','KMeans call does not pin n_init or init, and raw °C/% coordinates are used without scaling.','Record installed versions/defaults for author runs; avoid universal label-number or cross-version reproducibility promises; state raw-coordinate metric when computing distances.')
c=concept(815,'Zone output and cluster plot','Displayed labeling, centroids and new observation; arbitrary IDs and reproducibility.')
ask(c,'In the source’s displayed run, which mapping matches the annotated plot?', ['Zone 0: high Wi-Fi near 22 °C; Zone 1: moderate Wi-Fi near 25 °C; Zone 2: low Wi-Fi near 10 °C','Zone 0: low Wi-Fi near 10 °C; Zone 1: moderate Wi-Fi near 25 °C; Zone 2: high Wi-Fi near 22 °C','Zone 0: moderate Wi-Fi near 25 °C; Zone 1: high Wi-Fi near 22 °C; Zone 2: low Wi-Fi near 10 °C','Zone 0: high Wi-Fi near 22 °C; Zone 1: low Wi-Fi near 10 °C; Zone 2: moderate Wi-Fi near 25 °C'],['These are the source’s red zone annotations around the three groups.','This swaps the source’s labels 0 and 2.','This swaps the source’s labels 0 and 1.','This swaps the source’s labels 1 and 2.'],kind='diagram',assumptions=['Read the printed run’s annotations; cluster identifiers are not universal semantic labels.'])
ask(c,'Which legend interpretation matches the zone plot?', ['Small circles: exploration data; red X marks: centroids; magenta star: new reading','Small circles: centroids; red X marks: new readings; magenta star: all training data','Small circles: reward values; red X marks: failed actions; magenta star: policy','Small circles: labeled room names; red X marks: regression coefficients; magenta star: intercept'],['These are the displayed data, center and test markers.','This reverses the data and centroid roles and misassigns the single new reading.','No reward or policy is plotted.','The plot shows feature-space clustering, not regression parameters or supplied room labels.'],kind='diagram')
ask(c,'Suppose a second implementation produces identical groups but relabels the source’s cluster 0 as cluster 2. Does that alone mean the clustering is worse?', ['No; a consistent permutation of cluster IDs leaves the partition unchanged','Yes; label 0 always means the highest-quality cluster','Yes; cluster number must equal the zone_A/B/C generation order','No; labels can be changed independently for individual points without changing the partition'],['Integer IDs are names for groups, so a consistent renaming does not change membership relationships.','No quality ordering is encoded by an integer label.','Generation order need not determine fitted label order.','Changing individual labels inconsistently can change which points share a cluster.'],difficulty='medium')
ask(c,'For an illustrative nearest-center calculation, use fixed centers A=(10,20), B=(22,90), C=(25,40) in the source’s raw temperature/Wi-Fi coordinates. Which center is nearest to (23,85), and what is its squared-distance score?', ['B, with score 26','A, with score 4394','C, with score 2029','B, with score 6'],['For B, (23−22)²+(85−90)²=1+25=26, smaller than the other two scores.','A has score 13²+65²=4394, which is much larger.','C has score (−2)²+45²=2029, also much larger.','Six is the Manhattan distance |1|+|−5|, not the squared Euclidean score.'],kind='numerical',formula=[DIST],steps=['A: 13²+65²=4394.','B: 1²+(−5)²=26.','C: (−2)²+45²=2029.','The minimum is B; these supplied centers are population means, not exact finite-sample fitted centroids.'],assumptions=['Use the supplied mean centers as a simplified calculation, not the learned finite-sample centroids; raw numerical coordinates are used, so the score has no single physical SI unit.'],pages=[813,814,815])
c=concept(816,'Unsupervised robotics application table','Terrain grouping, anomaly detection, compact LiDAR and color compression with causal limits.')
ask(c,'Which application pairing matches the lecture’s table?', ['Clustering: group terrain from LiDAR; dimensionality reduction: compact LiDAR representation','Clustering: compute a shorter vector only; dimensionality reduction: return a terrain-group ID only','Clustering: maximize delayed reward; dimensionality reduction: label every torque fault','Clustering: fit a torque target; dimensionality reduction: execute a learned motor action'],['Grouping samples and shortening their representations are the two distinct roles in the table.','This swaps the defining outputs of the two tasks.','Rewards and supplied fault labels are not the listed unsupervised roles.','These are different supervised/control roles, not the table’s pairing.'],kind='match')
ask(c,'An unsupervised monitor flags a joint-torque pattern as unusual. What is the most defensible interpretation of the lecture’s mechanical-failure example?', ['It may indicate a problem requiring investigation, but an anomaly alone does not prove its cause','It proves mechanical failure even if operating conditions changed','It is necessarily a terrain cluster because torque is numerical','It proves the robot selected the optimal action sequence'],['The task detects abnormal patterns; causal diagnosis requires further context or evidence.','Novel operating conditions can also make patterns unusual, so the causal conclusion is too strong.','Numerical data can support anomaly detection as well as other tasks.','Anomaly detection is not an optimal-policy certificate.'],kind='scenario')
ask(c,'How does grouping similar pixel colors support the image-compression example?', ['Represent many similar colors with fewer representative colors, potentially reducing storage','Assign a separate new color to every original pixel, increasing the palette','Replace all color information with the robot’s reward history','Fit torque from mass and extension for each pixel'],['The table describes grouping similar colors to represent an image with fewer bits.','Expanding a palette is the opposite of the stated compression mechanism.','Reward histories do not encode the image colors in this example.','That is the regression task, not image compression.'])
persist()
c=concept(817,'Reinforcement-learning elements and objective','Agent/environment/state/action distinctions, reward timing and cumulative return with explicit horizon.')
ask(c,'For a robot learning navigation through trial and error, which role assignment is correct?', ['Learning decision-maker: agent; interacting world: environment; current situation representation: state','Learning decision-maker: state; interacting world: agent; current situation representation: reward','Learning decision-maker: environment; interacting world: reward; current situation representation: action','Learning decision-maker: action; interacting world: state; current situation representation: agent'],['These match the lecture’s core role definitions.','This confuses the learner with the state and reward.','This exchanges all three listed roles.','A chosen action is not the learner, and the environment is not just a state representation.'],kind='match')
ask(c,'A navigation controller can choose {left, right, forward} and now chooses left. Which terminology distinguishes the set from the selected move?', ['{left,right,forward} is the action set; left is an action','{left,right,forward} is one scalar reward; left is the state','{left,right,forward} is the chosen action; left is the entire action set','{left,right,forward} is a training target; left is a regression coefficient'],['An action is one choice from the available action set, not the entire set.','The moves are choices, not a reward or state description.','This reverses the selected element and the set of available elements.','These are not regression labels or parameters.'],conflict=(817,'Action (a) is defined as a set of possible moves.','Distinguish one action a from the set of available actions; the example explicitly separates set and chosen member.'))
ask(c,'The slide describes rewards as arriving only after a sequence of actions, while the next slide shows action then reward in a repeated loop. Which qualified statement accommodates both examples?', ['Rewards may be immediate or delayed; delayed consequences create a credit-assignment challenge','A reward after a single action cannot be used in reinforcement learning','Every RL task must reveal the correct action as a supervised label','Only the final action can influence a delayed reward'],['The loop permits stepwise feedback, while delayed effects explain why earlier actions may need credit.','The next source slide explicitly depicts action followed by reward.','A scalar reward evaluates outcomes rather than necessarily revealing the correct action.','Earlier actions can contribute to later outcomes.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[817,818],conflict=(817,'The text says rewards or penalties occur only after a sequence of actions.','Read this as a delayed-reward example, not a universal restriction; the next slide’s repeated action/reward loop allows stepwise feedback.'))
ask(c,'A controller interacts with a simulated navigation world rather than hardware. Is that excluded by the agent/environment interaction idea?', ['No; the relevant environment is what responds to the agent’s actions and supplies observations or rewards','Yes; an environment must be physical because state cannot exist in simulation','Yes; simulated actions are necessarily supervised target labels','No; any fixed unlabeled dataset automatically provides action-dependent environmental responses'],['The functional agent/environment distinction can be implemented in a simulator.','A simulator can represent state and respond to actions.','Simulation does not turn actions into supervised target labels.','A passive fixed dataset need not model responses to the learner’s chosen actions.'],kind='scenario',conflict=(817,'Environment is glossed as everything outside the agent, with physical world in parentheses.','The parenthetical is a robotics example, not a physical-only requirement; explicitly supply a simulator that responds to actions.'))
ask(c,'For a supplied two-step, undiscounted task, policy A deterministically earns rewards (5,−4) and policy B earns (1,3). Which policy maximizes cumulative reward over these two steps?', ['B, with total 4 rather than A’s total 1','A, because its first reward 5 exceeds B’s first reward 1','A, with total 9 rather than B’s total 4','B, with total 3 rather than A’s total −4'],['Sum both steps: A gives 5−4=1 and B gives 1+3=4.','Maximizing only the first reward ignores the specified two-step objective.','This incorrectly converts A’s penalty into a positive reward.','This compares final-step rewards only, not the cumulative totals.'],kind='numerical',formula=[fm('G=r₁+r₂',{'G':'two-step return','r₁,r₂':'first- and second-step rewards'},{'G,r₁,r₂':'dimensionless reward units'},['Exactly two steps; undiscounted sum; deterministic stated rewards; larger return preferred.'])],steps=['A: G_A=5+(−4)=1.','B: G_B=1+3=4.','Since 4>1, policy B has the larger cumulative reward.'],assumptions=['Two-step horizon, no discounting or additional costs beyond the listed deterministic rewards.'])
ask(c,'What distinguishes the lecture’s “learning with a critic” description from supervised learning with correct-answer labels?', ['Feedback evaluates consequences with rewards or penalties rather than necessarily naming the correct action','The critic must provide the correct class label for every action','The agent receives no feedback of any kind','The reward is always a complete description of the future optimal policy'],['Outcome evaluation can guide learning without an explicit correct-action target for each situation.','This is the teacher-style supervision the comparison distinguishes.','Rewards and penalties are feedback.','A reward signal is not itself a complete policy.'])
issue(817,'rl-terminology','Action is conflated with action set; reward timing is restricted to after sequences; environment is parenthetically called physical world.','Use explicit per-question qualification and conflict binding for each challenged phrase; preserve the source’s cumulative-reward and agent-interaction ideas.')
c=concept(818,'Reinforcement loop, challenges and applications','Observe/act/reward/update cycle, credit assignment, exploration/exploitation and named methods.')
ask(c,'Which order matches the reinforcement-learning loop diagram?', ['Observe state → take action → receive reward → update learner → repeat','Observe state → receive the resulting reward → take the action → update learner → repeat','Take action → update using its outcome → observe state → receive its reward → repeat','Observe state → take action → receive reward → repeat without updating the learner'],['This follows the diagram’s numbered steps and closing loop arrow.','The shown reward follows the action whose consequence it evaluates, not precedes it.','The displayed update follows receiving the reward rather than using an outcome not yet observed.','The diagram explicitly includes a learner-update step before repeating.'],kind='diagram')
ask(c,'A robot receives a success reward only after a ten-move route. Determining which earlier moves contributed most directly illustrates which challenge?', ['Credit assignment','Exploration versus exploitation','Choosing the current-state representation','Selecting the action set'],['Credit assignment connects delayed outcomes to the earlier decisions that helped produce them.','That concerns information-seeking versus using known choices, not attributing this delayed result to past moves.','State representation concerns how the current situation is encoded.','The available move set is distinct from attributing a later outcome to earlier decisions.'],kind='scenario')
ask(c,'Which choice correctly distinguishes exploration from exploitation?', ['Trying a less-known route to learn its outcome: exploration; choosing the best-known route: exploitation','Trying a less-known route: exploitation; choosing the best-known route: exploration','Both choices are exploitation whenever the robot moves','Both choices are exploration whenever a reward exists'],['Exploration seeks information; exploitation uses currently favored knowledge.','This reverses the roles.','Movement alone does not determine the exploration/exploitation distinction.','Rewards can accompany both information-seeking and best-known choices.'],kind='match')
ask(c,'Which set consists only of reinforcement-learning methods named on the slide?', ['Q-Learning, Sarsa, model-based learning','PCA, NMF, density-based spatial clustering','Linear regression, K-Means, PCA','Sarsa, NMF, linear regression'],['All three are listed in the slide’s Major Algorithms section.','Those are named under unsupervised categories elsewhere.','These belong to the supervised/unsupervised examples rather than this RL list.','Only Sarsa in this set is in the slide’s RL list.'],kind='statement')
ask(c,'Which setup most directly matches the slide’s RL applications of navigation and obstacle avoidance?', ['A robot selects movements and learns from progress/collision feedback over repeated interactions','A fixed collection of sensor vectors is grouped solely by similarity','A torque model is fitted to known mass-distance-torque examples','A scan is converted to fewer feature coordinates without choosing actions'],['This supplies actions and consequences for a navigation/avoidance task.','This is an unsupervised clustering setup.','This is supervised regression.','This is representation transformation rather than action learning.'],kind='scenario')
c=concept(819,'Problem formulation and paradigm selection','Task-relevant objectives, data availability and avoiding sophisticated solutions to the wrong problem.')
ask(c,'A team optimizes prediction accuracy for a quantity unrelated to the robot’s required task. What does the summary’s warning imply?', ['First check whether the target and evaluation criterion address the actual robotics problem','Add model complexity before checking whether the objective is relevant','Judge success solely by how many algorithms were combined','Replace evaluation with the amount of training time used'],['A technically accurate or complex model can still solve the wrong problem if its objective is misaligned.','Greater complexity does not repair an irrelevant objective by itself.','Algorithm count is not evidence of solving the right task.','Training effort is not a task-success criterion.'],kind='scenario')
ask(c,'Which statements match the summary’s task-and-data perspective? Select all that apply.', ['Known target outputs can motivate a supervised setup','Unlabeled sensor structure can motivate an unsupervised setup','Action-dependent rewards can motivate a reinforcement setup','The most complex model is always the right choice regardless of the objective'],['Supervised learning uses known target labels, as in the torque example.','Unsupervised learning can seek structure without target labels, as in the sensor example.','Reinforcement learning uses action consequences and rewards.','The summary explicitly warns that complex ML may solve the wrong problem.'],correct=(0,1,2),kind='synthesis',pages=[802,809,817,819])
persist()
